  "seoBody": "<div class=\"key-takeaways\"><strong>Key takeaways</strong><ul><li>UK food businesses must have a food safety management system based on HACCP principles under Regulation (EC) 852/2004 and the Food Safety Act 1990</li><li>Safer Food Better Business is the Food Standards Agency's ready-made system for small caterers; larger operators build a fuller HACCP plan</li><li>Temperature control, cleaning, allergens and opening and closing checks are the daily records an EHO expects to see</li><li>Documented records are your due-diligence defence and drive the confidence-in-management part of your Food Hygiene Rating</li><li>Add your whole team and only pay for who actually works: 20 pounds per active user a month, dormant users free, which suits a hospitality group across sites</li></ul></div><h2>What a food safety audit app is actually for</h2><p>Every UK food business, from a single cafe to a hotel group, must manage food safety. The Food Safety Act 1990, the Food Hygiene (England) Regulations 2013 and Regulation (EC) 852/2004 require a documented food safety management system based on HACCP principles, kept up to date and available for inspection. In practice that means daily checks, recorded, that an Environmental Health Officer can read.</p><p>An app does not make the kitchen safe. The practice does. What it does is make the daily check easier to complete correctly at the point of the task, harder to forget, and faster to surface to head office. That last point outweighs any feature list. The tool's job is to make on-the-spot recording the path of least resistance.</p><h2>The daily food safety records</h2><h3>1. Temperature control</h3><p>Chilled food is generally held at or below 8C, hot food at or above 63C, and cooking aims for a core temperature of 75C or an equivalent time and temperature combination. Fridge, freezer, cooking, hot-holding and cool-down readings are the backbone of the daily diary. They should be logged as they are taken, not reconstructed at close.</p><h3>2. Cleaning schedules</h3><p>A documented cleaning schedule shows what is cleaned, how often, by whom and with what. It is a standing part of any food safety management system and a routine EHO check.</p><h3>3. Allergen management</h3><p>Under the Food Information Regulations and Natasha's Law, the 14 major allergens must be managed, and prepacked for direct sale (PPDS) food must carry a full ingredients label with allergens emphasised. The record evidences that your controls were in place.</p><h3>4. Opening and closing checks</h3><p>Opening and closing checks tie the routine together: fridges on and cold, no pest signs, hand-wash stocked, waste clear. They are quick, frequent, and exactly the kind of check that gets skipped and backfilled without a tool that makes recording easy.</p><h2>HACCP, SFBB and your management system</h2><p>The law does not prescribe one format. It requires a system based on the seven HACCP principles: identify hazards, find critical control points, set limits, monitor them, take corrective action, verify, and keep records. Small caterers can adopt Safer Food Better Business, the FSA's ready-made pack with a daily diary. Larger operations build a fuller HACCP plan. Quickler captures the diary and monitoring records; it does not write your plan or replace a competent food safety adviser.</p><h2>Comparison: the main options</h2><ul><li><strong>Paper SFBB diary</strong> - the FSA pack. Free, familiar, but easy to backfill and impossible to see across sites.</li><li><strong>iAuditor</strong> - general inspection app, from around 19 pounds per seat per month. App install and template setup required.</li><li><strong>FoodDocs, Navitas</strong> - food-safety-specific apps. Capable, but another app and login, priced per seat or per site.</li><li><strong>Quickler</strong> - WhatsApp workflow, 20 pounds per active user per month, dormant users free. Only pay for who works. Free tier of 20 reports a month, everything unlimited on a paid account. Runs in WhatsApp, no install or training.</li></ul><p>Pricing is approximate and shifts. Check each vendor's current rates directly before you commit.</p><h2>What to look for in a food safety audit app</h2><ul><li><strong>On-the-spot completion:</strong> the tool should make it easier to record the check while doing it, not at close</li><li><strong>Photo and timestamp evidence:</strong> each reading tied to a time, a person and where useful an image</li><li><strong>Multi-site visibility:</strong> one head-office dashboard showing which site did what, today</li><li><strong>No per-seat penalty:</strong> per-seat pricing punishes groups for seasonal and office staff. Quickler charges 20 pounds per active user a month, dormant users free</li><li><strong>Due-diligence trail:</strong> a complete, timestamped record you can hand an EHO or produce in a dispute</li></ul><div class=\"callout\"><strong>Honest note:</strong> records do not make the kitchen safe, the practice does. But without the records you have no due-diligence defence and no confidence-in-management score. Quickler captures the checks with photo and timestamp and gives head office a live view across sites. It does not write your HACCP plan or replace a food safety adviser. <a href=\"/pages/help/index.html#contact-form-section\">Ask us</a> if that distinction matters for your firm.</div><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>What is a food safety audit app?</h3><p>It is any tool that helps hospitality staff record daily food safety checks and produce a report an EHO can read: temperature logs, cleaning schedules, allergen controls, opening and closing checks. Options range from paper diaries, to generic audit apps like iAuditor, to conversation-based tools like quickler that run the workflow over WhatsApp so there is no app to install.</p><h3>Is a food safety management system a legal requirement in the UK?</h3><p>Yes. Under the Food Safety Act 1990 and Regulation (EC) 852/2004, UK food businesses must put in place, implement and maintain a food safety management system based on HACCP principles, and keep records. Small caterers can use the FSA's Safer Food Better Business pack. Always check current FSA guidance for your business type; this is not legal advice.</p><h3>Can I run food safety checks over WhatsApp?</h3><p>Yes. Quickler's workflows run over the WhatsApp Business API. Staff receive each check as a message in their existing WhatsApp chat, reply with text, a voice note or a photo, and the record generates automatically with a timestamp. No separate app or login is required, and quickler manages the WhatsApp Business API account on the firm's behalf.</p><h3>Does an app improve my Food Hygiene Rating?</h3><p>Not by itself. The rating reflects hygiene, structure and confidence in management. What good records do is evidence the confidence-in-management element and give you a due-diligence defence. The kitchen still has to run well. The app captures that it did, with photos and timestamps head office can see across every site.</p><h3>Related guides</h3><ul><li><a href=\"/pages/haccp-checklist-app-uk.html\">HACCP checklist app</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/kitchen-temperature-log-app-uk.html\">Kitchen temperature log app</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/due-diligence-food-hygiene-uk.html\">Food hygiene due diligence records</a></li></ul>",
